\section{Introduction}
\subsection{The Problem}
Our goal is the development of a cost-effective, scalable, and durable method for the separation of biological nanoparticles from aqueous solutions. Current methodologies, such as centrifugation and ultracentrifugation, are often inefficient, costly, and require high sample volumes. This project addresses this gap by designing a high-performance microfluidic platform that utilizes surface tension and microchannel geometry to isolate particles efficiently.

\subsection{The Mechanism}
The core mechanism relies on the observation that differences in surface tension between the nanoparticle surface and surrounding media create a driving force for particle transport. By designing microchannels with hydrophobic regions and hydrophilic regions, the system exploits the meniscus curvature to force the particles along the desired path. The surface tension gradient is generated by the macroscopic movement of the fluid through the channel, which interacts with the particle's surface properties.

\subsection{Design Considerations}
To achieve high throughput and minimize energy consumption, the system must operate at low Reynolds numbers. The microchannels are etched using photolithographic technology, allowing precise control of channel depth, width, and hydrophilic/hydrophobic regions. The optical system employs wavelength-selective filters to ensure optimal light scattering and absorption characteristics for the desired particle size range.

\subsection{Validation}
We validate the separation performance using a commercial fluorescent bead mixture. The results demonstrate separation efficiency, showing a separation efficiency of ~90% for the smallest particles in the sample. The data also shows minimal background scattering from the micro-channel walls.

\section{Conclusion}
This microfluidic platform represents a major advancement in nanoparticle manipulation. By addressing the limitations of traditional separation techniques, we can significantly reduce the cost of these materials. Future work includes scaling up the device to accommodate larger volumes without sacrificing separation accuracy.

